WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.250
- If they put shoulder pads
and cleats on Usain Bolt

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.167
it would be Randy Moss.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:06.917
- The big reason everybody’s
on Trubisky is cuz he was

00:00:06.917 --> 00:00:09.500
drafted in the same class
as Mahomes and Watson.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.958
- I know you know football, you’ve
been watchin’ the Jets all season.

00:00:11.958 --> 00:00:12.958
- I don’t know if that’s football.

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:14.250
♪♪♪

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.708
♪♪♪

00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:20.042
- Yo, what up?
It is Simms.

00:00:20.042 --> 00:00:21.000
- And Lefkoe.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.250
- Appreciate you.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.208
- One thing that we really
care about

00:00:24.208 --> 00:00:26.708
is not just talking
about the NFL

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:29.375
but talking about things
that people seem to get wrong.

00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:30.833
- Right.
- We see it all the time.

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:32.708
- Setting the narrative straight.
- Yeah.

00:00:32.708 --> 00:00:34.375
- Yeah.
- It really upsets you.

00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:36.542
- It does drive me crazy.
- Why does it upset you so much?

00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:37.875
- Well, because a lot of the times

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:39.708
the narrative is false,
I'm finding out.

00:00:39.708 --> 00:00:42.708
It's lazy homework done—
- Yes.
- By people in the media

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.833
where they just make a take,
and I don't know

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.625
if they have any useful
information to back it up.

00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:49.000
- I'm gonna set the grains.
- Yeah.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.708
- You go against them.
- OK.

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:54.833
- Earlier in the year and still now—
- Right.

00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:56.667
- I see people doubtin'
Mitchell Trubisky.

00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.292
- Ohh.
- Starts off, "Oh, Seattle game

00:00:58.292 --> 00:00:59.708
Green Bay game."

00:00:59.708 --> 00:01:01.625
There was a notion
that Mitchell Trubisky

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:04.292
couldn't throw left—
- Yes.
- For a few weeks this year.

00:01:04.292 --> 00:01:05.958
- Right.
- That was a popular notion.

00:01:05.958 --> 00:01:08.167
What's the truth?
What's the "against the grain"?

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:11.125
- The big reason everybody's
on Trubisky more than anything

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:13.042
is cuz he was drafted
in the same class

00:01:13.042 --> 00:01:15.833
as Mahomes and Watson,
and everybody wants that.

00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:17.500
- It's tough.
- And that's not fair.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.958
Mahomes got drafted to a team
that was a playoff team.

00:01:19.958 --> 00:01:22.083
Watson essentially
got drafted to a team

00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:23.708
that was playoff-caliber—
- Had a lot of injuries.

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.833
- But had injures to their
quarterback and to other players.

00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:27.625
- Trubisky was a true
top-three-pick team.

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:29.208
- A top-three-pick team.

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:32.125
He's got talent, and he's got
talent around him

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:33.958
and that's why
I can't draw a line in the sand

00:01:33.958 --> 00:01:35.875
and just go, "He stinks."
That's not fair.

00:01:35.875 --> 00:01:37.750
- One team that does not have talent?
- Right.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.542
- The Buffalo Bills,
Sean McDermott.

00:01:39.542 --> 00:01:41.583
- Right.
- Three wins thus far.

00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:43.792
What's Sean McDermott doing?

00:01:43.792 --> 00:01:45.000
You almost think he should be up

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.750
for the Coach of the Year
candidacy.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.542
- He should be
in the conversation

00:01:48.542 --> 00:01:50.542
for what
Sean McDermott is doing.

00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:52.167
- Against the grain.
- Yeah.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:54.875
I mean, first of all,
this team is not good.

00:01:54.875 --> 00:01:57.792
The other thing is,
he's a defensive expert.

00:01:57.792 --> 00:01:59.542
They're the No. 1 defense
in football.

00:01:59.542 --> 00:02:01.500
Do you know how hard it is to be
the No. 1 defense

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.125
in football
when you don't have

00:02:03.125 --> 00:02:04.875
an offense or a quarterback
that can do anything?

00:02:04.875 --> 00:02:06.833
- When your quarterbacks have
been Peterman and Derek Anderson—

00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:08.000
- Exactly.
- For a majority of the season?

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.375
- And a rookie, Josh Allen.

00:02:09.375 --> 00:02:11.083
They have no receivers on the outside.
- Yeah.

00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:14.500
- He manages the game perfectly on
a weekly basis, and I know

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.333
he won't win Coach of the Year.
- Yes.

00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:17.917
- But he deserves
to be in the convo.

00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:19.750
- Can I go against the grain?
- Of course you can.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.042
- Uh, I'm seein' a lot of people
right now goin'

00:02:22.042 --> 00:02:24.792
"Oh, Mahomes is good,
but Brees is the MVP.

00:02:24.792 --> 00:02:26.458
It's really, like

00:02:26.458 --> 00:02:27.833
it's the one thing
he doesn't have in his career.

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:29.083
He's really earned it
at this point.

00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:30.542
Mahomes is gonna
have more time."

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:33.250
This is not a lifetime achievement award.
- Yes, exactly.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.333
Drew Brees is not playing better
than Patrick Mahomes.

00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:38.625
Drew Brees cannot complete
half the passes

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:39.000
Drew Brees cannot complete
half the passes

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.792
that Patrick Mahomes does.

00:02:40.792 --> 00:02:43.583
If you watch the game against
the Bengals—
- Sure.

00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:45.042
- Dump-off to Kamara—
- Right, screens.
- Dump-off to Ingram.

00:02:45.042 --> 00:02:47.708
- Right.
- I would argue that, probably around 40 percent

00:02:47.708 --> 00:02:50.125
of Drew Brees' yards,
are screens, dump-offs

00:02:50.125 --> 00:02:51.792
and slants
that get taken to the house.

00:02:51.792 --> 00:02:53.333
- And Drew Brees is awesome.
We're not being haters.

00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:54.833
- He's great.
- But what, to me

00:02:54.833 --> 00:02:56.792
and I think you're
on the right track here

00:02:56.792 --> 00:02:59.792
is when you're in the MVP conversation—
- You're a game-changer.

00:02:59.792 --> 00:03:02.208
- You are the person
that makes the team go.

00:03:02.208 --> 00:03:04.042
- People are hangin' off you,
and you're throwing touchdowns.

00:03:04.042 --> 00:03:05.875
- Exactly.
Mahomes makes the team go.

00:03:05.875 --> 00:03:08.792
- Yes.
- Todd Gurley makes the Rams go.

00:03:08.792 --> 00:03:10.750
- Yes, yes.
- I would argue on the Saints

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.083
the O-line and the running back
tandem make the team go.

00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:16.708
- So you would put Andrus Peat
in the MVP conversation?

00:03:16.708 --> 00:03:19.708
- Well, I mean, their offensive
line and their run game

00:03:19.708 --> 00:03:21.583
and then, of course,
Michael Thomas, so I can't sit

00:03:21.583 --> 00:03:23.708
there and go, "Drew Brees
is the No. 1 reason

00:03:23.708 --> 00:03:25.125
that's making this
team successful."

00:03:25.125 --> 00:03:26.708
- Yeah.
- It's a combination of things

00:03:26.708 --> 00:03:28.833
and, of course,
he's still playing really good.

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:29.958
- Uh, you've been noticing

00:03:29.958 --> 00:03:31.583
a trend of me lately.
- Yes.

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:33.500
- And we're fixing it today.
- Good.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:34.917
- Yeah.
- Let's do that.

00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:36.042
Hey, what's up, slugger?

00:03:36.042 --> 00:03:37.500
Ooh.
Welcome to grown-up-hood.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.792
- See how that happened.
- Whoo!

00:03:39.792 --> 00:03:41.375
- Don't touch me.
- [Laughing]

00:03:42.375 --> 00:03:44.792
- Swag: Nate Burleson has it
by the boatload.

00:03:44.792 --> 00:03:46.083
It's flowing.
- Thank you.

00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:47.792
- He's got purposeful dirt
on his jeans.
- [Laughing]

00:03:47.792 --> 00:03:50.833
- That's incredible.
I've never seen that before.

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:52.500
He's been making fun
of me lately

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.417
because every guest we have,
there's an issue

00:03:55.417 --> 00:03:56.833
with my handshakes.
- Uh-huh.

00:03:56.833 --> 00:03:58.208
- And I don't wanna
show it to you.

00:03:58.208 --> 00:03:59.875
I need your help

00:03:59.875 --> 00:04:01.458
but I need you to see
what's been happening lately.

00:04:01.458 --> 00:04:03.292
- Let's break this down.

00:04:03.292 --> 00:04:04.417
- Pleasure.
- Likewise.

00:04:04.417 --> 00:04:07.125
Definitely.
It was fun.

00:04:07.125 --> 00:04:10.458
- Again, check out
Martellus' show.

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:10.500
- Again, check out
Martellus' show.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.125
- Westbrook, you're the man.
- Thank you.

00:04:13.125 --> 00:04:14.458
- Appreciate it.
- You the man.

00:04:14.458 --> 00:04:16.042
- Did you see that I went
with the pound?

00:04:16.042 --> 00:04:17.542
- Yeah, he's afraid now.
- Because I was intimidated.

00:04:17.542 --> 00:04:18.917
- He didn't know which—yeah.
- No, it's just—

00:04:18.917 --> 00:04:20.250
- He's afraid.
- I've had, I've just had—

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.917
my handshakes haven't
been good lately.

00:04:21.917 --> 00:04:23.167
- Listen, first of all—
- Yeah?

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:24.500
- You just gotta
make eye contact.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.167
- OK, OK, OK.
- That's all it is.

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:27.833
All right? And then you gotta
square your shoulders.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:29.042
You know what I'm sayin'?
I mean, for you.

00:04:29.042 --> 00:04:30.333
- Yeah, yeah.
- Cuz for my guy Chris

00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:31.583
if I see him, it's like,
"What's up, baby?

00:04:31.583 --> 00:04:32.833
How you doin'?
Good to see you."

00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:34.583
You know what I'm sayin'?
It's real Chris. That's all it is.

00:04:34.583 --> 00:04:36.333
- Yes.
- But like, with you, you gotta

00:04:36.333 --> 00:04:38.042
like, square the shoulders.
- Square.
- I think you gotta make

00:04:38.042 --> 00:04:39.750
a little eye contact. Not too
much, cuz we're not

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.083
making out.
- OK, how high am I going

00:04:41.083 --> 00:04:42.417
with the hand?
- Yeah, don't go too high.

00:04:42.417 --> 00:04:44.042
Bring it about shoulder-high,
right?
- OK.

00:04:44.042 --> 00:04:46.000
- And then you wanna make sure to look
at the elbow.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.917
You know what I'm sayin'?
- Oh, you look at the elbow?

00:04:47.917 --> 00:04:49.875
- YOU need to look at the elbow.
- So I abandon eye contact—

00:04:49.875 --> 00:04:51.208
- Just know where I'm goin'
with it

00:04:51.208 --> 00:04:52.833
cuz, I mean,
if I got a little sauce

00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:54.333
and I've known you for a while,
I can come over here

00:04:54.333 --> 00:04:55.708
and be like,
"What's up, baby?"

00:04:55.708 --> 00:04:57.042
- Ah s--t.
- That's gonna throw you way off.

00:04:57.042 --> 00:04:58.667
- I'm scared.
All right, do it again.

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:00.500
- I'm gonna keep it right here.
- Eye contact, square

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.833
and then focus on the elbow.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:03.083
- All right, so—
- Yeah.

00:05:03.083 --> 00:05:04.792
- What's up, Lef?
- Oh, what's up, man?

00:05:04.792 --> 00:05:06.375
- How you doin'? You good?
All right.

00:05:06.375 --> 00:05:08.292
I don't need you to lick
your lips like LL Cool J.

00:05:08.292 --> 00:05:10.583
- Damn. I did do that.
- [Laughing]

00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:12.458
- All right, hold on. Say—
- That was good, though.

00:05:12.458 --> 00:05:14.083
- Good contact.
Good confidence.

00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:15.333
- Yeah.
- Then what?

00:05:15.333 --> 00:05:17.250
Because I have done this
for a long time.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:17.542
Because I have done this
for a long time.

00:05:17.542 --> 00:05:18.583
- I don't like that.

00:05:18.583 --> 00:05:19.875
So listen, we come in,
we come here.

00:05:19.875 --> 00:05:21.292
Boom.
- Yes.

00:05:21.292 --> 00:05:22.542
- And then release,
and it has to be straight

00:05:22.542 --> 00:05:24.000
when we come here, though.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.208
I saw you come here.
- It was not my fault.

00:05:25.208 --> 00:05:26.458
- Yeah, cuz you might as well

00:05:26.458 --> 00:05:27.875
just go ahead and kiss
his hand like he's royalty.

00:05:27.875 --> 00:05:29.125
- Yes, yes.
- But you know what I'm saying?

00:05:29.125 --> 00:05:30.292
You straighten it up.
Bam.

00:05:30.292 --> 00:05:31.542
- All right.
- All right.

00:05:31.542 --> 00:05:33.000
- All right, man.
I hope I see Nate today.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.625
- Oh, there he is!

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:36.458
- Oh, Lefkoe. What up, man?
How are you, dude?

00:05:36.458 --> 00:05:37.958
- You did it.
- No!

00:05:37.958 --> 00:05:39.250
Listen.
I was tryin' to grab it

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.875
and you slid out,
and my finger popped up.

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:42.625
- All right. Maybe that's why
I've been doing it.

00:05:42.625 --> 00:05:44.292
- Listen. All of our fingers
have to connect. These four.

00:05:44.292 --> 00:05:45.958
- All right. Last one. Last one!
- Five on five, here.

00:05:45.958 --> 00:05:47.333
You can do it.

00:05:47.333 --> 00:05:48.583
- I'm really nervous.
- Hey, Lef.

00:05:48.583 --> 00:05:49.750
What up, baby?
- Oh, what up, man?

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:51.250
How are you doin'?
- You good?
- I'm good.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.042
- Ohhh!
- Here we go! That's it. That's it.

00:05:53.042 --> 00:05:55.375
And listen, if you ever worry,
you can always hold on

00:05:55.375 --> 00:05:56.875
to it for a beat.
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:05:56.875 --> 00:05:58.542
- Really?
- Yeah, man.

00:05:58.542 --> 00:06:00.375
So you good? How's the fam?
- Oh, that's too much.
- Oh, man, good to see you, man.

00:06:00.375 --> 00:06:02.083
- And then I just let go.
- Yeah, you just let go.

00:06:02.083 --> 00:06:03.750
Cuz, like—
- Thank you.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.375
- If you don't know whether
it's gonna be a snap, pop

00:06:05.375 --> 00:06:06.833
or an extra hit—some guys
like to blow it up afterward.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:07.917
You can just hold it
and be like

00:06:07.917 --> 00:06:09.500
"Yo. What's up? You good?"
- I like that.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.042
- "Hey, I appreciate you having me
on the show, man.

00:06:11.042 --> 00:06:12.333
Next time, we gotta talk
some politics."

00:06:12.333 --> 00:06:13.833
Like that.
- I'm a snapper.

00:06:13.833 --> 00:06:15.833
I'm a snapper at the end
because I went to Texas.

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:17.625
- Yeah, of course.
- Bam.

00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:19.583
- You know what I'm saying?
- I'll get to that eventually.

00:06:19.583 --> 00:06:22.042
- That's sauce all day, damn.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:23.125
- I'm sitting between, I think

00:06:23.125 --> 00:06:24.625
- I'm sitting between, I think

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:27.708
the two most employed people
in NFL media.

00:06:27.708 --> 00:06:29.792
Radio shows, TV shows—
- We're workin'.

00:06:29.792 --> 00:06:31.875
- But I mean, what's it like
for both of you guys?

00:06:31.875 --> 00:06:34.833
- Once you leave the game,
you realize

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:36.208
"All right, I gotta bust my ass

00:06:36.208 --> 00:06:38.375
and work just as hard
in my new craft."

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:40.958
So, like, every opportunity
is like me being a rookie again

00:06:40.958 --> 00:06:42.250
and when I was a rookie

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:43.875
I walked in
to the Minnesota Vikings

00:06:43.875 --> 00:06:47.958
and I'm seein' Daunte Culpepper
in a Theismann fur

00:06:47.958 --> 00:06:50.792
and I'm seein' Randy Moss
swagged out with a baggy suit

00:06:50.792 --> 00:06:53.167
and I'm like,
"Damn, this is the NFL."

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:55.167
These dudes pullin' up in
Bentleys with spinners on it.

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:57.167
- Yeah.
- And I'm like—

00:06:57.167 --> 00:06:58.750
- In Minnesota.
- "I'm a baby. I'm a baby

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.375
and I gotta work."

00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:02.250
Not only to make a name
for myself

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.167
but to, like,
get these guys' attention

00:07:04.167 --> 00:07:06.667
and that was my goal,
to get the greats' attention.

00:07:06.667 --> 00:07:09.667
So, like, what we do now
is if we gotta work one

00:07:09.667 --> 00:07:12.625
two, three, four jobs, we want
to get attentions of the masses

00:07:12.625 --> 00:07:13.750
cuz we're still workin'.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.083
- I do wanna jump off

00:07:15.083 --> 00:07:16.250
of something you said
a few minutes ago.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:17.875
- Oh, Randy Moss?
- Yeah.

00:07:17.875 --> 00:07:20.250
- I was gonna say, you
experienced young Randy Moss.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.208
- Right.
- You experienced old Randy Moss.

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:23.500
- Right.
- The first time

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:26.750
I met Randy Moss,
I knew immediately that

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:30.542
if I got an "attaboy" pat
on the back from him

00:07:30.542 --> 00:07:32.958
I've arrived as a wide receiver.

00:07:32.958 --> 00:07:34.375
But I went about it
the wrong way

00:07:34.375 --> 00:07:36.042
because I was tryin'
to get his attention

00:07:36.042 --> 00:07:38.542
like a thirsty dude
at the end of the night.

00:07:38.542 --> 00:07:40.208
Like, I was like, "All right,
as soon as I hit him

00:07:40.208 --> 00:07:41.625
with this icebreaker,
we're gonna be

00:07:41.625 --> 00:07:43.417
best friends forever, man."
- [Laughing]

00:07:43.417 --> 00:07:46.208
And I remember one day, I was
getting ready for practice

00:07:46.208 --> 00:07:48.375
and I think
it was Jermaine Dupri.

00:07:48.375 --> 00:07:51.292
He had a music video, and he's
wearin' the Marshall 88 jersey.

00:07:51.292 --> 00:07:53.500
So I'm like in my little
dorm room at training camp.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.333
I was like, "This is it, man."

00:07:55.333 --> 00:07:56.958
And I remember,
I was doing conditioning

00:07:56.958 --> 00:07:58.417
and I'm standing next to him,
and I'm literally

00:07:58.417 --> 00:08:00.167
like, gassin' myself
up like a dude

00:08:00.167 --> 00:08:02.500
about to walk across the bar
and ask a girl for their number.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:03.917
I was like, "All right, man.
Come on.

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:05.542
Don't blow it, don't blow it."

00:08:05.542 --> 00:08:08.125
And I was like, "Hey, uh, Randy, um."
Voice was crackin'.

00:08:08.125 --> 00:08:09.750
- Yeah.
- I don't know what was goin' on.
I'm a grown man.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.667
And I was like, "Did you see
the Jermaine Dupri video?

00:08:12.667 --> 00:08:14.625
He had your jersey on, man."

00:08:14.625 --> 00:08:15.833
And he's runnin' next to me.

00:08:15.833 --> 00:08:17.292
He looks at me

00:08:17.292 --> 00:08:18.708
and he just keeps on runnin'.

00:08:18.708 --> 00:08:21.375
I was crushed!
- Ohhh!
- I was like, "Ugh."

00:08:21.375 --> 00:08:24.500
Listen, I wrote a girl a note,
"Do you like me, yes or no?"

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.000
in the fifth grade,
and she crumpled it up

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.458
and threw it at my face.

00:08:27.458 --> 00:08:30.458
- Oh, man.
- Randy Moss hurt me more.

00:08:30.458 --> 00:08:32.167
Like, my feelings were crushed,
and I was like

00:08:32.167 --> 00:08:34.000
"Damn, I blew it."

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.500
And then what's crazy is, like,
once I started makin' plays

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.500
like, that's when he came in
and was like, "All right."

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:39.917
Like, "I know you love the game

00:08:39.917 --> 00:08:41.458
and I know you're one
of the good ones."

00:08:41.458 --> 00:08:41.625
and I know you're one
of the good ones."

00:08:41.625 --> 00:08:43.208
- It's funny, I think,
to hear him say that

00:08:43.208 --> 00:08:44.708
because I don't think people
always realize

00:08:44.708 --> 00:08:46.667
that football players
can also be fanboys.

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:48.708
First time Randy Moss
came on the plane

00:08:48.708 --> 00:08:52.042
I was Row 27 by the window,
and he said, "Can I sit here?"

00:08:52.042 --> 00:08:53.167
I was like [stuttering]

00:08:53.167 --> 00:08:54.750
"Yeah, sure, sure.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.125
You can sit here."
- Yeah, yeah.

00:08:57.125 --> 00:08:58.625
- And, I mean,
that's the way I felt

00:08:58.625 --> 00:09:00.625
but like Nate's sayin', too

00:09:00.625 --> 00:09:02.833
the one thing I learned from him
in a hurry

00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:06.542
is his perspective
on the game was, like, amazing.

00:09:06.542 --> 00:09:08.667
He could almost see the game
almost like a quarterback.
- Yeah.

00:09:08.667 --> 00:09:12.167
- He understood offense
and defense, and, you know

00:09:12.167 --> 00:09:14.083
he understood
when this play was stupid

00:09:14.083 --> 00:09:15.833
and he wasn't gonna
tell the coach

00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:17.667
"That's a stupid play, Simms,"
you know, whatever it was.
- Yeah.

00:09:17.667 --> 00:09:20.292
- I mean, and I just, I really
enjoyed being around him.

00:09:20.292 --> 00:09:20.833
- I mean, and I just, I really
enjoyed being around him.

00:09:20.833 --> 00:09:22.958
- Calvin is the forgotten guy.
- Yeah.

00:09:22.958 --> 00:09:25.250
- If I'm creating a player
in "Madden"

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:29.208
he's 6'5" running a 4.3,
and his name is Calvin Johnson.

00:09:29.208 --> 00:09:30.875
- Yeah.
- But he's never mentioned

00:09:30.875 --> 00:09:34.708
in the "Jerry Rice, T.O.,
Randy Moss" top three.

00:09:34.708 --> 00:09:36.667
But I would argue
he is physically as gifted

00:09:36.667 --> 00:09:38.125
as any of them, if not more.

00:09:38.125 --> 00:09:39.667
- No question.
I mean, it's the same individual

00:09:39.667 --> 00:09:41.292
that broke Jerry Rice's
single-season record.
- Yeah.

00:09:41.292 --> 00:09:43.250
And I watched him do it.
- Right.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:46.208
- I watched games where
he would put up a buck 90

00:09:46.208 --> 00:09:48.958
and we go to the meeting,
and after a loss

00:09:48.958 --> 00:09:50.625
Shawn Jefferson,
who's a former player

00:09:50.625 --> 00:09:52.792
wide receiver—he was
our wide receivers coach

00:09:52.792 --> 00:09:54.958
He'd say, "Calvin, I need you to do more."
- [Laughing]

00:09:54.958 --> 00:09:58.000
And, you know, I'm thinkin'
like, "Damn, what you want?

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.667
The dude go for 250?"
- Seriously.
- Yeah.

00:09:59.667 --> 00:10:01.083
- And Calvin wouldn't
say anything.

00:10:01.083 --> 00:10:02.750
He was quiet.

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:05.000
You know, his mother, a doctor.
Dad, he worked on

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.417
the railroad,
so he had this blend

00:10:06.417 --> 00:10:08.667
of blue-collar and high IQ

00:10:08.667 --> 00:10:10.458
and that was his approach
to life

00:10:10.458 --> 00:10:13.083
and he would just nod his head
and say, "All right, Coach."

00:10:13.083 --> 00:10:14.583
Next game, go put up 300.

00:10:14.583 --> 00:10:17.833
Now, Randy was just pure speed
encapsulated.

00:10:17.833 --> 00:10:21.375
If they put shoulder pads
and cleats on Usain Bolt

00:10:21.375 --> 00:10:24.583
and he was actually a good
football player that can catch

00:10:24.583 --> 00:10:27.167
and track the ball like no
other, it would be Randy Moss.
- Yeah.

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:29.000
- And Randy Moss had the greatest
late hands ever.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.750
Even though coaches say, "Don't
panic, remember your cues

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.083
don't give anything away,"
like, we panic.

00:10:34.083 --> 00:10:35.917
Like, we don't remember our
cues, and we give everything away.

00:10:35.917 --> 00:10:37.750
Like, when the ball's
in the air

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.792
you start hearing your own
breath, and you go from—

00:10:40.792 --> 00:10:42.500
[Panting]

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:43.875
And if you're
a really good DB

00:10:43.875 --> 00:10:45.167
I know it's a small detail

00:10:45.167 --> 00:10:46.625
but you can hear
a guy breathing hard

00:10:46.625 --> 00:10:49.583
and then you see his eyes
go from here to here—
- Yes.

00:10:49.583 --> 00:10:51.375
- And that right there is a cue
that something's coming.

00:10:51.375 --> 00:10:53.125
- Right.
- Randy would, like, look

00:10:53.125 --> 00:10:56.250
and the ball would be comin',
and his face doesn't move

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.000
his hands doesn't move,
his breath doesn't move

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.083
and then right when it gets
to him, he's like—

00:11:00.083 --> 00:11:01.708
Velcro.

00:11:01.708 --> 00:11:04.458
Like, that's something
that you might not see ever.

00:11:04.458 --> 00:11:06.083
- No.
- I don't think so.

00:11:06.083 --> 00:11:07.875
- And that's not something you coach.
It's something that God said

00:11:07.875 --> 00:11:09.708
"Hey, Randy, I got
a little extra for you."

00:11:09.708 --> 00:11:12.458
- Right. You the man.
- That was awesome.

00:11:12.458 --> 00:11:13.792
- Nate Burleson's the man.
- He is the man.

00:11:13.792 --> 00:11:14.125
- Nate Burleson's the man.
- He is the man.

00:11:14.125 --> 00:11:15.333
- I think we're gonna
have him back again.

00:11:15.333 --> 00:11:16.458
I really do.
- Good.

00:11:16.458 --> 00:11:17.833
- You know who else is the man?

00:11:17.833 --> 00:11:20.542
Every person on the Rams
and Chiefs roster.

00:11:20.542 --> 00:11:23.333
It is the game of the year.
It is the fourth time

00:11:23.333 --> 00:11:26.542
since, like, 1990 that two 9-1 teams

00:11:26.542 --> 00:11:28.417
have faced off
at this point of the season.

00:11:28.417 --> 00:11:30.250
- Yep.
- How good of a game will this be?

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.625
Will this be 40-40?
Will it be disappointing?

00:11:32.625 --> 00:11:34.292
What's the Simms prediction?

00:11:34.292 --> 00:11:36.375
- Well, you know what?
In games like this

00:11:36.375 --> 00:11:38.750
more times than not, they don't
live up to the billing

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:41.292
or the expectations
of, like, a shootout, right?

00:11:41.292 --> 00:11:42.292
- This year's been different,
though.

00:11:42.292 --> 00:11:43.708
Rams-Saints was really good.

00:11:43.708 --> 00:11:45.333
- I know, I know.
I guess I always—

00:11:45.333 --> 00:11:46.500
- Chiefs-Patriots
was really good.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.917
- It was extremely good.

00:11:47.917 --> 00:11:49.750
If you made me bet,
I'm going to say

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:51.250
we're going to get a shootout.
- Whoo!

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:54.417
- We're gonna get a 38-35 type
of football game.

00:11:54.417 --> 00:11:56.208
But what happens a lot of times
in these matchups, too

00:11:56.208 --> 00:11:57.958
defenses try different
things too

00:11:57.958 --> 00:11:59.667
cuz they go, "Man, we can't
really stop this group.

00:11:59.667 --> 00:12:00.833
We gotta do something different."

00:12:00.833 --> 00:12:01.917
- Which sounds awesome.
- Yes.

00:12:01.917 --> 00:12:03.208
- Sounds like 70-yard touchdowns.

00:12:03.208 --> 00:12:04.542
- But it can also make
the offense

00:12:04.542 --> 00:12:06.375
lose their rhythm too,
just goin'

00:12:06.375 --> 00:12:08.250
"Oh, they've never played this
defense versus this coverage."

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.042
But either way, I expect a ton
of big-time plays

00:12:11.042 --> 00:12:12.708
because it's good play design—
- Yeah.

00:12:12.708 --> 00:12:15.000
- And there's talent
on both sides of the ball.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.000
- And I like that both defenses
take risks—
- Yeah.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:18.542
- Which will allow things deep.
- Exactly right.

00:12:18.542 --> 00:12:20.458
- Our question, though,
for this game—
- Right.

00:12:20.458 --> 00:12:23.042
- Rams, Chiefs:
Better Big Three.

00:12:25.042 --> 00:12:27.458
I think the hardest part about
this is picking your Big Three.

00:12:27.458 --> 00:12:29.042
- Ooh!
- I look at the Chiefs.

00:12:29.042 --> 00:12:31.875
Is your Big Three Mahomes,
Kareem Hunt and Hill?
- Right.

00:12:31.875 --> 00:12:34.042
- Is it Mahomes, Kareem Hunt
and Kelce?
- Yes, right.

00:12:34.042 --> 00:12:35.792
- Is it Mahomes, Kelce and Hill?
- Right.

00:12:35.792 --> 00:12:37.375
Who would be your Big Three
for the Chiefs?

00:12:37.375 --> 00:12:38.750
- Who are you picking?
- Oh my gosh

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:40.167
that's really hard.

00:12:40.167 --> 00:12:42.208
I don't even know
which—I mean, gosh.

00:12:42.208 --> 00:12:45.375
- All right, well, I'm gonna go
Goff, Gurley and Cooks.

00:12:45.375 --> 00:12:46.875
- Cooks is for sure.
- That's set.

00:12:46.875 --> 00:12:48.292
- For sure. I mean, yes.

00:12:48.292 --> 00:12:49.542
- Who are your Big Three
for the Chiefs?

00:12:49.542 --> 00:12:51.000
I gave you, like,
six seconds there.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.417
- Yeah, OK.
I mean, it's obviously Mahomes

00:12:52.417 --> 00:12:54.208
and Tyreek Hill.
- OK.
- And I'm gonna go Kareem Hunt as the third.

00:12:54.208 --> 00:12:56.208
- I would have gone Kelce. That's an awful decision.
- And I'm sorry, Travis Kelce.

00:12:56.208 --> 00:12:59.375
You're really awesome. I know.
- Of those two Big Threes

00:12:59.375 --> 00:13:00.958
who you goin' with?

00:13:00.958 --> 00:13:03.292
- I am gonna go with the Chiefs
in a close one.

00:13:03.292 --> 00:13:04.750
- If I had to make the argument

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:06.208
for the Rams,
cuz I agree with you.

00:13:06.208 --> 00:13:08.000
I would take Mahomes
over anybody.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.208
- Yep.
- Mahomes.

00:13:09.208 --> 00:13:11.500
- Yes.
- Gurley is, I believe

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:13.792
the best of all
the six players out there.

00:13:13.792 --> 00:13:15.333
- OK.
I believe you there.

00:13:15.333 --> 00:13:16.833
- I believe that Gurley
is the No. 1 pick—
- Yes.

00:13:16.833 --> 00:13:19.000
- If we're not valuing
quarterbacks more.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.583
I think he's the most special.
- OK.

00:13:20.583 --> 00:13:22.833
- He's also the guy,
other than Tyreek Hill, that

00:13:22.833 --> 00:13:26.125
when he touches the ball, I go,
"What's gonna happen right now?"

00:13:26.125 --> 00:13:27.458
- Yeah, you do that
with Mahomes too.

00:13:27.458 --> 00:13:28.208
So don't forget that.
- Yeah, you're right.

00:13:28.208 --> 00:13:29.000
So don't forget that.
- Yeah, you're right.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.625
Hi, I'm Justin Forsett

00:13:31.625 --> 00:13:33.917
and you're watching Simms and Lefkoe.

00:13:34.792 --> 00:13:36.042
- Guys, I'm embarrassed.

00:13:36.042 --> 00:13:37.708
Ever since "The Dragon"
left me hanging

00:13:37.708 --> 00:13:40.958
somebody hasn't picked
a game right for one, two

00:13:40.958 --> 00:13:42.250
three weeks in a row.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:44.083
That's not acceptable
for somebody

00:13:44.083 --> 00:13:45.500
that likes Lefkoe Locks.

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:48.500
Frankly, we're locked out.
Locked out.

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:50.750
Sean, call a locksmith!

00:13:53.583 --> 00:13:57.792
We are in a historic place
right here, AAA Locksmiths.

00:13:57.792 --> 00:13:59.833
You've been here in Midtown
for how long, Dave?

00:13:59.833 --> 00:14:01.708
- 80-plus years.
- 80-plus years.

00:14:01.708 --> 00:14:04.333
We're talkin' changing locks
for Mickey Mantle

00:14:04.333 --> 00:14:06.333
lead singer of KISS, who else?

00:14:06.333 --> 00:14:07.667
Joe Montana?

00:14:07.667 --> 00:14:10.167
- Barbra Streisand,
Harry Winston.

00:14:10.167 --> 00:14:11.625
- I'm a man of locks.

00:14:11.625 --> 00:14:13.250
I'm a man of Lefkoe Locks

00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:15.417
and we have lost three
"Thursday Night Football"

00:14:15.417 --> 00:14:17.583
games in a row, Dave,
and I know you know football.

00:14:17.583 --> 00:14:19.250
You've been watchin' the Jets
all season.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:20.333
- I don't know
if that's football.

00:14:20.333 --> 00:14:21.542
- That's a good point.

00:14:21.542 --> 00:14:23.500
But these two teams
know football

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:25.792
Seattle, Green Bay in Seattle

00:14:25.792 --> 00:14:27.583
Seahawks favored by 2.5.

00:14:27.583 --> 00:14:30.333
I hear you got a gambling guy
that you asked for a tip.

00:14:30.333 --> 00:14:31.875
What did he say?

00:14:31.875 --> 00:14:34.417
- He says you gotta go
with the road dog here.

00:14:34.417 --> 00:14:36.708
- Who's their quarterback, Dave?
- Aaron Rodgers.

00:14:36.708 --> 00:14:38.250
- Aaron Rodgers.

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.083
So, it's 2.5 points.

00:14:40.083 --> 00:14:42.333
Are you confident in this?
- I'm quite confident.

00:14:42.333 --> 00:14:46.667
- Are you confident enough
to make it a Lefkoe Lock?

00:14:46.667 --> 00:14:49.375
- Yes.
- It's been locked.

00:14:49.375 --> 00:14:53.375
Dave lockin' it up,
takin' the Packers on the road

00:14:53.375 --> 00:14:55.208
plus the points, 2.5.

00:14:55.208 --> 00:14:57.125
- Dave the Rave.
- Aaron Rodgers and 2.5 points

00:14:57.125 --> 00:14:58.500
sounds pretty good.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:00.458
- Yeah, I mean, I don't know.

00:15:00.458 --> 00:15:02.833
It's "Thursday Night Football,"
they're on the road in Seattle

00:15:02.833 --> 00:15:04.875
and what scares me—
I'm gonna go

00:15:04.875 --> 00:15:07.083
I'm goin' Seahawks, OK?
- OK.

00:15:07.083 --> 00:15:08.667
- I'm goin' against Dave the Rave.
Sorry. Negative, Ghost Rider.

00:15:08.667 --> 00:15:11.375
But, yeah, I think the run game
of the Seahawks

00:15:11.375 --> 00:15:13.625
against a not-so-good
run defense

00:15:13.625 --> 00:15:16.625
of the Green Bay Packers—
- At home.
- That scares me a little bit.

00:15:16.625 --> 00:15:18.208
- If you take away
the season opener

00:15:18.208 --> 00:15:20.042
cuz that's not teams
playing on a short week

00:15:20.042 --> 00:15:23.375
the home teams are 7-2,
the average margin of victory

00:15:23.375 --> 00:15:26.208
for the "Thursday Night
Football" games is 18 points.

00:15:26.208 --> 00:15:27.833
They're not even games.

00:15:27.833 --> 00:15:30.042
Simms, I'm seeing these guys
complain on Twitter

00:15:30.042 --> 00:15:31.625
the winners,
like the Steelers—
- Yeah.

00:15:31.625 --> 00:15:33.875
- Saying, "I'm still not right
from the last game."

00:15:33.875 --> 00:15:35.542
- Right.
- These games stink.

00:15:35.542 --> 00:15:37.083
- They stink.
- How do we fix this?

00:15:37.083 --> 00:15:39.125
What's the issue?
- OK, well, the biggest issue

00:15:39.125 --> 00:15:41.208
first of all,
is the coaching, right?

00:15:41.208 --> 00:15:43.917
It's a Thursday game,
so there's not—

00:15:43.917 --> 00:15:46.125
The coaches lose the chance
to be creative

00:15:46.125 --> 00:15:47.833
and add new things
into their game plan.

00:15:47.833 --> 00:15:49.458
You don't get a
lotta practice time.

00:15:49.458 --> 00:15:50.667
Coaches aren't comfortable—

00:15:50.667 --> 00:15:51.792
- I've heard a lot
of coaches say

00:15:51.792 --> 00:15:53.083
"We're just gonna
keep it simple."

00:15:53.083 --> 00:15:54.458
- Exactly.
That's what they're gonna do.

00:15:54.458 --> 00:15:55.542
- So that's why it's not
a great game.

00:15:55.542 --> 00:15:56.958
- Well, yeah, exactly right.

00:15:56.958 --> 00:15:59.042
- Were you sore?
- I was sore, certainly.

00:15:59.042 --> 00:16:01.958
I mean, I'm a wussy quarterback,
and on Thursday mornings

00:16:01.958 --> 00:16:03.375
I woke up still sore

00:16:03.375 --> 00:16:05.000
after starting a football game on Sunday.
- Wow.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.000
- So imagine how Bobby Wagner
feels or Rashaad Penny

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:11.083
or any other defensive player that's
had to make a lot of big, you know—

00:16:11.083 --> 00:16:13.333
- Hits.
- Contact, car crash-type hits.

00:16:13.333 --> 00:16:14.750
- Something needs to happen.
- It does.

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:16.000
- Very much so.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.333
- For Simms.
- Peace out, homies.

00:16:17.333 --> 00:16:18.583
- I don't know why
I'm so serious right now.

00:16:18.583 --> 00:16:20.667
The L-E-F-K-O-E.
- Man.

00:16:20.667 --> 00:16:22.833
